    id: "partner-living-together",
    situation: "A close friend has moved in with a partner without marrying, and you hold the conviction that sex belongs within marriage.",
    accept: "Keep being their friend. Visit their home, welcome both of them at your table, learn the partner's name and story, and celebrate the good things in their life. Do not treat the partner as a threat or a project. Remember that your friend is far more than this one choice. Ask about their work, their family, their hopes and their struggles. If they have a wedding one day, or a breakup, or a baby, be the friend who shows up. Jesus was known for being at people's tables before he was known for his opinions.",
    reject: "You do not have to pretend you share their view, and you do not have to give a speech either. If they ask what you think, answer honestly and briefly, and make clear that your friendship is not on the line. You may choose for your own household what you will and will not host. Paul says plainly that his concern for sexual ethics was for the church, not for policing outsiders: “what have I to do with also judging those who are outside?” (1 Cor 5:12). If your friend is a Christian who asks for your counsel, you may speak more directly; if not, your witness is mainly your life and your love. Either way, your conviction can be honest without being a condition.",
