    accept: "See the person, not just the problem. Tell them they are loved and worth fighting for. Stay in contact, notice good days, and treat them with dignity even on bad days. Addiction is often tangled with pain, shame and sometimes trauma. Speak to the person underneath it: “I miss you. I believe in you. I'm here when you want help.” Recovery usually involves relapses, so let your love be steady rather than conditional on a perfect record. Celebrate small steps honestly.",
    reject: "Do not fund, cover up or make excuses for the addiction. Refusing to give money, to lie to employers, or to clean up every consequence is not cruelty; it is refusing to help the thing that is hurting them. You may also refuse to let them drive your children or stay in your home while using. Paul distinguishes the crushing burden (<em>baros</em>) we help one another carry (Gal 6:2) from the ordinary load (<em>phortion</em>) each person must carry (Gal 6:5). Helping carry a crisis is love; taking over someone's responsibility for their own choices usually keeps the addiction going. Saying no to enabling is saying yes to the person.",
