  {
    id: "other-religion-friend",
    situation: "A good friend practises another religion: Muslim, Hindu, Jewish, Buddhist or something else.",
    accept: "Honour your friend and their sincerity. Learn about their faith from them rather than from stereotypes, respect their practices in your home (food, prayer times, holy days), and enjoy what you share. Paul in Athens began by noticing what was good and searching in the city's religion, even quoting their poets: “in him we live, and move, and have our being” (Acts 17:28). You can likewise appreciate what is true and beautiful in your friend's tradition, and let them see that you value them as a friend, not as a target.",
    reject: "You do not have to pretend all religions are the same, and they probably would not want you to. You can say gently that you believe Jesus shows us who God is, while respecting their freedom to disagree. Never use friendship as a sales technique. Respect also means letting them be fully themselves. Ask whether they would like to hear about your faith before you share it. Be honest that Christians believe Jesus is “the way, the truth, and the life” (John 14:6), and honest too that in the relational gospel this is good news of a God whose love embraces all people, not a weapon. If your friend comes to a hard time, such as illness, grief or loss, be there in practical ways, and if they welcome it, offer to pray for them. Many deep conversations about God begin not in debate but at a hospital bed or a kitchen table.",
    handle: [
      "Ask what their faith means to them and listen well.",
      "Share your own experience of Jesus when it fits naturally, as a witness, not a debate.",
      "Find common ground in serving others together.",
      "Pray for them as a friend, and let love, not fear, shape your conversations."
    ],
    refs: ["Acts 17:22-28", "1 Pet 3:15", "Luke 10:25-37", "John 14:9"]
  },
  {
    id: "church-gossip",
    situation: "A member of your church keeps spreading gossip about others.",
    accept: "See their need, perhaps for belonging, importance or control, and continue to treat them as a brother or sister. Do not respond by gossiping about the gossiper. Gossip often comes from insecurity or a longing to be included and important. You can meet that need in a better way: spend time with them, involve them in serving, and speak words that build them up. People who feel secure in love have less need to tear others down.",
    reject: "Refuse to listen to or pass on the stories. Change the subject, or say kindly, “I'd rather not talk about them when they're not here.” Gossip divides friends (Prov 16:28), and declining to receive it is one of the simplest ways to stop it. “For lack of wood a fire goes out. Without gossip, a quarrel dies down” (Prov 26:20). You may need to say to the gossiper, gently and directly, “I don't think this is ours to share. Have you talked to them?” If the gossip damages someone's reputation, it may be right to stand up for the person being spoken about.",
    handle: [
      "Gently decline to engage with gossip each time it comes.",
      "If it continues, talk with them privately about the harm it causes.",
      "If it seriously damages people, involve a wise leader, following the steps of Matthew 18.",
      "Model the alternative: speak well of people, and speak to people rather than about them."
    ],
    refs: ["Prov 16:28", "Prov 26:20", "Eph 4:29", "Matt 18:15-17"]
  },
  {
    id: "hurt-apologised",
    situation: "Someone hurt you badly, and they have sincerely apologised.",
    accept: "Receive the apology and forgive, as God has forgiven you. Release the debt and your right to punish them. Celebrate their courage in owning what they did. Receiving an apology well is a gift. Try not to minimise it (“oh, it was nothing”) if it wasn't nothing, and try not to punish it by using the moment to list every other fault. A simple “Thank you. I forgive you” can open the way to real healing for both of you.",
    reject: "Forgiveness is not saying it did not matter, and it is not the same as instantly restored trust. You can forgive fully and still rebuild trust step by step, watching for changed behaviour over time. Jesus links forgiveness to repentance in Luke 17:3-4, and even says to forgive repeatedly. But he does not say to trust repeatedly without wisdom. If the harm was serious (for example a betrayal of trust), it is reasonable to agree on what rebuilding will look like: openness, time, perhaps help from a counsellor. If the person repeats the same harm again and again, forgiveness still holds, but the relationship may need firmer limits. Repeated apologies without change are a reason for wisdom, not for cynicism.",
    handle: [
      "Tell them you forgive them, and mean it, even if feelings take longer.",
      "Be honest about what you need now (time, space, different behaviour).",
      "Let trust grow in proportion to the change you see.",
      "Refuse to keep bringing the past back as a weapon."
    ],
    refs: ["Eph 4:32", "Luke 17:3-4", "Col 3:13", "Prov 20:11"]
  },
  {
    id: "hurt-no-apology",
    situation: "Someone hurt you badly and has never apologised or admitted it.",
    accept: "You can still choose to forgive in your heart, releasing them to God and refusing to let bitterness rule you. You can still wish them good and pray for them, as Jesus prayed for those who crucified him. Forgiving someone who has not apologised is not pretending. It is choosing to hand the debt to God, who judges justly (1 Pet 2:23), so that the hurt does not keep owning you. It may help to remember that the person who hurt you is also loved by God, broken in ways you cannot see, and not beyond his reach.",
    reject: "Forgiveness without repentance does not mean reconciliation. Without acknowledgement, a restored relationship may not be possible or wise. You may keep distance, limit contact and protect yourself and others. Reconciliation is a two-way street; forgiveness is something you can offer alone, but a restored relationship requires truth from both sides. “As much as it is up to you” (Rom 12:18) means some peace is not yours to produce. Where there was abuse, keeping distance is wise, and you never owe anyone access to you because you have forgiven them.",
    handle: [
      "Bring the hurt honestly to God, including the anger; the Psalms show this is allowed.",
      "Forgive as an act of release, even if it has to be repeated many times.",
      "Decide on wise boundaries for contact, based on safety and their behaviour, not guilt.",
      "Seek support from trusted friends or a counsellor if the wound runs deep."
    ],
    refs: ["Luke 23:34", "Rom 12:17-21", "Ps 55", "Rom 12:18"]
  },
  {
    id: "political-relative",
    situation: "A relative holds political views you find wrong or even harmful, and family gatherings get tense.",
    accept: "Remember they are family and made in God's image, not a symbol of the other side. Look for their real concerns and fears, which are often understandable even when their conclusions are not. Enjoy what you share beyond politics. Your relative may have reached their views through experiences, fears or loyalties you do not share. Getting curious about those (“What worries you most about this?”) often lowers defences on both sides. Look for ways to love them that have nothing to do with politics: help with a task, share a meal, ask about their memories.",
    reject: "You do not have to agree, stay silent about real injustice, or let yourself be belittled. You may decline to debate at the dinner table and choose another time, or none at all. You can name real harm without contempt. Jesus spoke strongly against injustice and also loved people on every side of the political struggles of his day: among his twelve were a tax collector who had worked with Rome and a Zealot who wanted Rome gone (Matt 10:3-4; Luke 6:15). If a conversation becomes cruel, you may calmly end it. Remember too that you may be wrong about some things, and that your relative may be right about some things. Humility does not weaken your convictions; it makes them easier to hear.",
    handle: [
      "Agree, if possible, on topics or times when politics is off the table.",
      "When you do talk, ask questions and seek to understand before replying.",
      "Speak clearly about harm to real people, without contempt for your relative.",
      "Remember that your first citizenship is in a kingdom that unites people across every divide."
    ],
    refs: ["Phil 3:20", "Jas 1:19-20", "Col 4:6", "Gal 3:28"]
  },
  {
    id: "different-doctrine",
    situation: "A fellow Christian holds doctrines you think are wrong, perhaps about hell, the end times, gifts of the Spirit or the atonement.",
    accept: "Welcome them as a brother or sister in Christ. Assume good faith. Recognise that serious, Jesus-loving people have disagreed about many of these questions for centuries. The relational gospel itself holds some views that many Christians strongly disagree with, so it should be especially quick to extend the welcome it would want to receive. Christians who hold penal substitution, who believe in eternal conscious punishment, or who read the end times differently are still brothers and sisters, and many of them love Jesus deeply.",
    reject: "You may explain why you see things differently and stand firm on what you believe Scripture teaches. You do not need to join practices that violate your conscience. But do not treat secondary differences as reasons to break fellowship. You may disagree openly and even firmly; Paul and Barnabas disagreed so sharply they parted ways for a time (Acts 15:39), and later Paul spoke warmly of Barnabas' cousin Mark (Col 4:10; 2 Tim 4:11). Strong disagreement is not the same as division. Refuse caricatures, and give others' views their best wording before responding.",
    handle: [
      "Distinguish core convictions (such as the Creed) from secondary disputes.",
      "Understand their view well enough to state it in their best terms.",
      "Discuss with humility and a willingness to learn, not to win.",
      "Keep praying, serving and eating together; unity in love is itself a witness."
    ],
    refs: ["Rom 14:1-5", "Eph 4:1-6", "John 17:21", "1 Cor 13:12"]
  }
